  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nThis afternoon, broadly zonal flow was present over the Northern\nPlains, with a Hudson Bay low to the northeast and a secondary\nclosed low just offshore of the Pacific Northwest. A shortwave\naloft associated with the Hudson Bay low was over the central\nCanadian Prairies. At the surface, broad high pressure extended\nfrom east central Alberta into northwest North Dakota. \n\nAn embedded vort max in the mean flow has been producing light\nradar returns across southwest North Dakota throughout the day\nso far today, although we have to see any confirmation of any\nrain making it to the surface, which is not too surprising given\nthe high ceilings. Northwest winds are a bit breezy with the\nsurface pressure gradient, but will be diminishing through the\nrest of the day as the surface high continues to push across the\nstate. Highs this afternoon will be in the upper 50s to upper\n60s. Lows tonight will be seasonably chilly, mainly in the upper\n30s to mid 40s, although we could see some mid 30s across the\nInternational Border. \n\nWe did contemplate whether or not a Frost Advisory would be needed \ntonight. Although the surface high moving in would typically \nfavor temperatures dropping well below guidance, latest \ndeterministic guidance is advertising a shortwave diving south \nfrom the Canadian Prairies overnight tonight. Recent CAMs runs \nare all in agreement on scattered precipitation across at least \ncentral North Dakota tonight, with forecast RH and BUFKIT \nsoundings showing midlevel clouds extending across the forecast \narea. This, plus current satellite imagery showing widespread \ncloud cover upstream across southern Alberta and Saskatchewan, \nhas us wondering how much clouds will clear out even as the \nsurface high moves through and subsequently how much \ntemperatures will drop below the deterministic NBM which is \nalready skewed close to the 25th percentile. Some isolated \nfrost is still possible mainly across our four northwest \ncounties and into Renville and Bottineau Counties, but at this \npoint we are not confident enough in widespread impacts to go \nahead with an advisory. Forecasters this evening will re- \nevaluate.\n\nOn Sunday, the aforementioned deep closed low will continue to \nmove onshore into the Pacific Northwest, with downstream \nshortwave ridging across the Northern Plains that will warm \ntemperatures some for Sunday and Monday. Highs rebound back \ninto the lower 60s to lower 70s on Sunday, and then the mid 60s \nto mid 70s on Monday. As the low approaches the region, expect \nwidespread showers and occasional thunderstorms to start late \nSunday evening across western North Dakota, expanding east \nthrough the night and Monday. NBM PoPs continue to be quite high\nespecially for areas along and east of Highway 83, with a rainy\nMonday morning commute expected. This is the dividing line of a\n50/50 chance for more than 0.50 inches of rain (east) and less\nthan that (west). Showers and thunderstorms will end rather \nquickly from west to east through the day Monday as the upper \nlow lifts northeast.\n\nThe main threat will be for localized heavy rain, with PWATs \nforecast around 1.5 inches which is towards the upper end of \nclimatology for mid September. We are also expecting strong\nwinds with this system, starting southeasterly on Sunday and\nswitching to westerly on Monday behind the front that moves\nthrough. ECMWF EFI highlights southwest North Dakota as the area\nof most concern with a shift of tails across much of the\nforecast area, so current thought is wind gusts of 50 to 55 mph\nacross western North Dakota on Monday. \n\nWe quickly return to zonal flow on Tuesday, with highs in the\n60s but lighter winds after the early week system. Moderate\nchances for rain return on Wednesday as a progressive embedded\nwave moves in as synoptic flow turns more southwesterly across\nthe region. NBM temperature percentiles signal a return to near\nnormal temperatures by the end of the work week.",
